1
00:00:02,730 --> 00:00:04,930
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:06,160
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:11,690
My name is Catherine and today we have a new lesson in our series about interviewing for a new job.

4
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:13,810
This is of course at the elementary level.

5
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:14,590
That's right.

6
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:17,210
So this is part four of our interview series.

7
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:25,330
Remember we are taking a look each week at giving you tips and also the proper language for when you have an interview in English.

8
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:28,330
And on this occasion we're going to talk about our work experience.

9
00:00:28,810 --> 00:00:34,150
Okay, you remember we talked a little bit about educational experience and educational background.

10
00:00:34,710 --> 00:00:36,970
Well today is all about your last couple of jobs.

11
00:00:37,770 --> 00:00:44,030
Alright, so I think we should just listen to the dialogue for the first time and we'll be back in a bit with language takeaway.

12
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:55,480
Right Rebecca, now I see that after graduating from university your first job was...

13
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:57,800
For a local paper in York called the York Herald.

14
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:00,470
Actually I started with them as an intern.

15
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:05,870
I was really keen on getting some experience in journalism and this seemed like a good first step.

16
00:01:06,210 --> 00:01:06,590
Certainly.

17
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:09,070
And after your internship?

18
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:13,290
They seemed impressed and offered me a position as a junior local news reporter.

19
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:15,970
I ended up staying two years there actually.

20
00:01:16,570 --> 00:01:18,930
I was in charge of the sports news section of the newspaper.

21
00:01:19,610 --> 00:01:22,310
I really enjoyed it there and it really helped me build my skills.

22
00:01:22,870 --> 00:01:27,030
Yes I see, but you decided to leave them in 2006, right?

23
00:01:27,330 --> 00:01:28,110
Yes that's right.

24
00:01:28,530 --> 00:01:33,750
My husband and I moved to London and so I managed to find a position with a national newspaper based in London.

25
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:35,920
The London Weekly, right?

26
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:36,920
Yes.

27
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:41,930
In some ways it was a step down from my previous job but it did offer me much better prospects for the future.

28
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:52,390
Alright we're back so now we've picked out a couple of key words and we're going to take a look at those now on Language Takeaway.

29
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:55,840
Language Takeaway.

30
00:01:56,680 --> 00:02:00,760
So Marco, a lot of people don't start out as a professional with a job.

31
00:02:00,760 --> 00:02:01,960
They have something else first.

32
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:02,960
What is this?

33
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:03,960
That's right.

34
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:09,140
A very popular way to begin your work experience, you become an intern or you work as an intern.

35
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:12,740
Okay so the name of the job we could say is intern.

36
00:02:13,180 --> 00:02:15,700
I'm an intern or he's an intern at my company.

37
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:16,240
Exactly.

38
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:19,680
So usually interns work at a company.

39
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:31,290
Usually they're not paid or if they are it's a very, very little pay and the purpose of an internship or to work as an intern is to gain experience.

40
00:02:31,710 --> 00:02:32,050
That's right.

41
00:02:32,250 --> 00:02:36,070
So it's basically like a study opportunity for a job that you know you want.

42
00:02:36,810 --> 00:02:43,410
I could be an intern at a media company and I could learn all about media but it's not really a full job.

43
00:02:43,630 --> 00:02:44,010
Exactly.

44
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:50,540
So it's really it serves as an opportunity to maybe continue working at that company in a full time job.

45
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:58,310
So you become an intern and the noun or what you are doing is called an internship.

46
00:02:58,790 --> 00:03:02,730
So I'm an intern but what I have is an internship.

47
00:03:03,790 --> 00:03:04,950
It's like saying I have a job.

48
00:03:05,130 --> 00:03:07,760
I have an internship but I am an intern.

49
00:03:08,020 --> 00:03:08,280
Exactly.

50
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:10,920
Alright so we've covered those two words.

51
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:22,580
Now let's move on to when she's describing her work experience in the York Herald and she described it as a very good first step.

52
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:27,360
Okay so a first step you think about your foot, your step, your moving.

53
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:32,100
It means the very first thing you do when you're heading in some direction.

54
00:03:33,120 --> 00:03:36,820
Right and professionally I guess your first steps are maybe becoming an intern.

55
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:46,300
Right or you could say my first step was working in the mail room at this big movie studio but later I became a famous movie producer.

56
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:52,420
Exactly so that's kind of like the way that you begin your professional life.

57
00:03:53,640 --> 00:04:02,330
And well then they started talking about the newspapers and actually Rebecca was offered a position as a junior local news reporter.

58
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:08,730
Okay so junior means usually lower ranking or not experienced.

59
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:13,670
Someone who is just starting out and of course after junior we have senior.

60
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:18,890
So a senior news reporter is someone who's worked for many many years as a reporter.

61
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:24,610
Usually I guess a junior news reporter would make less money than a senior news reporter.

62
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:29,070
Exactly you could also say junior executive or senior executive.

63
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:30,230
Junior manager.

64
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:33,670
These are ways of describing length of experience.

65
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:37,410
And in this case she's junior but what kind of job does she have?

66
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:41,200
Well she is a news reporter for local issues.

67
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:43,370
Okay so she's a local news reporter.

68
00:04:43,670 --> 00:04:48,190
Now what exactly does that mean if you're a local news reporter or for local issues?

69
00:04:48,650 --> 00:04:55,090
Well if I live in Chicago and I'm a local news reporter I only write or report the news about Chicago.

70
00:04:56,350 --> 00:04:58,870
It's a way of describing the things in my area.

71
00:04:59,550 --> 00:05:03,150
So it's the opposite of our next phrase national news reporter.

72
00:05:03,370 --> 00:05:14,270
Okay so if you are a national news reporter or a national manager of maybe a company then that means that you oversee things in the whole country.

73
00:05:14,670 --> 00:05:22,190
That's right so local is my city or my county but in the case of national we're talking about the whole country.

74
00:05:22,750 --> 00:05:23,510
Nation national.

75
00:05:24,070 --> 00:05:25,390
So national is an adjective.

76
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:27,880
But we could even go further than this.

77
00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:30,750
What if I am a reporter and I talk about foreign countries?

78
00:05:31,050 --> 00:05:34,270
I talk about France and South Africa and even Iceland.

79
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:38,280
Then I guess you would be an international news reporter.

80
00:05:38,280 --> 00:05:39,280
Exactly.

81
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:42,970
And I guess also at the executive level you can have an international marketing manager right?

82
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:44,680
That's right.

83
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:47,090
International marketing manager, national marketing manager.

84
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:54,930
These are very important ways to divide work to say he is the international manager, he is the national manager.

85
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:59,150
This tells you the kind of the size of this person's job.

86
00:05:59,370 --> 00:05:59,670
Exactly.

87
00:06:00,230 --> 00:06:09,490
Okay so now more or less you know how these jobs can be divided junior, senior, national, local, etc.

88
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:13,040
So I think we should go back to the dialogue.

89
